steps {
  type: CORTEX_STEP_TYPE_USER_INPUT
  status: CORTEX_STEP_STATUS_DONE
  metadata {
    created_at {
      seconds: 1795115433
      nanos: 452110441
    }
    source: CORTEX_STEP_SOURCE_USER_EXPLICIT
    source_trajectory_step_info {
      trajectory_id: "15cd0c2a-6930-4583-af9d-f24139d9c637"
      step_index: 1
      cascade_id: "49f9382e-a5a8-4ac9-ad38-8127f285cebb"
    }
    model_api_content_id: "8cfd3f42c5d94633988d9230c404397d"
  }
  user_input {
    user_response: "# STARFALL \342\200\224 Comprehensive Arcade Flight Feel Optimization\n\n## Project Context\n\nSTARFALL is a browser-based third-person 3D arcade space combat game.\n\nThe current project has been rolled back to a working baseline. Mouse input and the basic reticle behavior work, but aiming at moving enemies remains difficult.\n\nThe user wants traditional arcade space combat controls with precise aiming, smooth reticle recentering, and fast directional turning.\n\nThis task authorizes a comprehensive flight-feel optimization across the related control systems.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nCreate a coherent control experience that supports both:\n\n1. Precise, stable aiming at moving enemies.\n2. Fast, continuous directional turning.\n\nDo not optimize these as isolated features. They must work together.\n\n## 1. Mouse Input Architecture\n\nInspect and preserve the working Pointer Lock implementation.\n\nRequirements:\n\n- Use `movementX` and `movementY` for relative mouse input.\n- Preserve physical screen-edge continuity.\n- Do not use cursor recentering.\n- Process every input delta exactly once.\n- Separate raw mouse input, virtual reticle position, steering demand, and ship angular velocity.\n\n## 2. Precision Aiming\n\nImprove center-region precision.\n\nRequirements:\n\n- Small mouse movements must produce subtle and predictable steering.\n- Reduce overshoot during small corrections.\n- Avoid excessive sensitivity near the center.\n- Make repeated left/right corrections stable.\n- Preserve sufficient responsiveness for moving-target tracking.\n\nImplement a continuous, monotonic nonlinear response curve.\n\nAvoid abrupt response transitions.\n\n## 3. Progressive Steering\n\nSteering strength should increase progressively with mouse displacement from the virtual center.\n\nRequirements:\n\n- Low response near the center.\n- Gradually increasing response through the middle region.\n- Strong response near the outer region.\n- Independent yaw and pitch calibration.\n- Configurable maximum turn rates.\n\nDo not solve precision problems by globally reducing sensitivity.\n\n## 4. Reticle Boundary and Rebound\n\nRetain the rounded-rectangle reticle boundary and smooth self-centering behavior.\n\nRequirements:\n\n- The reticle must not instantly snap to the center.\n- Rebound should feel smooth and predictable.\n- Tune rebound speed and damping to avoid distracting oscillation.\n- The reticle must remain visually constrained.\n- Rebound animation must not accidentally reset or cancel ship steering.\n- Reaching the boundary must not create sudden steering spikes.\n\nThe reticle\'s visual position and steering demand must remain separate state variables.\n\n## 5. Angular Velocity and Damping\n\nReview how steering demand becomes ship rotation.\n\nRequirements:\n\n- Avoid excessive angular inertia.\n- Avoid sluggish acceleration.\n- Reduce unwanted rotation after input stops.\n- Support smooth directional reversal.\n- Prevent oscillation during fine corrections.\n\nUse separate controls for steering response and angular damping.\n\nDo not add unnecessary smoothing.\n\n## 6. Camera Coordination\n\nInspect the existing chase camera.\n\nEnsure:\n\n- Camera response is coordinated with ship rotation.\n- No excessive follow lag.\n- No camera motion that makes the reticle appear unstable.\n- Preserve the existing camera architecture unless a specific defect is identified.\n\n## 7. Settings and Calibration\n\nOrganize related tuning values in a centralized configuration.\n\nExpose useful settings where appropriate:\n\n- Base sensitivity.\n- Response curve.\n- Maximum yaw rate.\n- Maximum pitch rate.\n- Rebound speed.\n- Rebound damping.\n- Angular damping.\n- Yaw/pitch balance.\n\nUse sensible initial values based on the existing implementation and measured behavior.\n\nDo not claim values come from commercial games unless verified.\n\nAvoid exposing unnecessary technical controls in the player-facing Settings panel.\n\n## 8. Performance and Code Quality\n\n- No React state updates every frame for flight physics.\n- Use refs or engine state for real-time updates.\n- Avoid unnecessary allocations in the frame loop.\n- Keep input, steering, reticle, camera, and rendering responsibilities separated.\n- Preserve existing combat, weapons, bots, missions, and progression systems.\n\n## 9. Required Testing\n\nRun automated tests and actual browser gameplay tests where possible.\n\nTest all of the following:\n\n1. Tiny mouse corrections around the center.\n2. Slow tracking of a moving enemy.\n3. Repeated small corrections without overshooting.\n4. Approaching the reticle boundary.\n5. Smooth reticle rebound.\n6. Continuous steering during and after rebound.\n7. Large directional turns.\n8. Rapid left/right reversals.\n9. Stopping mouse input.\n10. Horizontal and vertical aiming independently.\n11. 30, 60, and 120 FPS behavior.\n\nPay particular attention to the interaction between rebound and steering demand.\n\n## 10. Acceptance Criteria\n\nThe optimization is complete only when:\n\n- Fine aiming is substantially easier.\n- Moving-target tracking feels stable.\n- Reticle rebound is smooth.\n- Rebound never interrupts steering.\n- Large turns remain fast.\n- Small corrections do not cause excessive rotation.\n- Yaw and pitch feel consistent.\n- No physical mouse-edge input interruption.\n- No regressions to existing gameplay systems.\n\n## Execution Instructions\n\nInspect the current implementation first.\n\nThen implement the complete related optimization in one coordinated pass.\n\nYou are authorized to inspect and edit relevant project files, run tests, and use browser testing tools available in the environment.\n\nDo not rewrite the entire game or replace the flight architecture.\n\nPreserve the rolled-back baseline so the complete optimization can be reverted.\n\nAt completion, report:\n\n- Root causes identified.\n- Files changed.\n- Control model implemented.\n- Exact final tuning values.\n- Automated test results.\n- Actual browser playtest results.\n- Known limitations.\n\nNever describe simulated tests as real mouse gameplay validation."
    items {
      text: "# STARFALL \342\200\224 Comprehensive Arcade Flight Feel Optimization\n\n## Project Context\n\nSTARFALL is a browser-based third-person 3D arcade space combat game.\n\nThe current project has been rolled back to a working baseline. Mouse input and the basic reticle behavior work, but aiming at moving enemies remains difficult.\n\nThe user wants traditional arcade space combat controls with precise aiming, smooth reticle recentering, and fast directional turning.\n\nThis task authorizes a comprehensive flight-feel optimization across the related control systems.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nCreate a coherent control experience that supports both:\n\n1. Precise, stable aiming at moving enemies.\n2. Fast, continuous directional turning.\n\nDo not optimize these as isolated features. They must work together.\n\n## 1. Mouse Input Architecture\n\nInspect and preserve the working Pointer Lock implementation.\n\nRequirements:\n\n- Use `movementX` and `movementY` for relative mouse input.\n- Preserve physical screen-edge continuity.\n- Do not use cursor recentering.\n- Process every input delta exactly once.\n- Separate raw mouse input, virtual reticle position, steering demand, and ship angular velocity.\n\n## 2. Precision Aiming\n\nImprove center-region precision.\n\nRequirements:\n\n- Small mouse movements must produce subtle and predictable steering.\n- Reduce overshoot during small corrections.\n- Avoid excessive sensitivity near the center.\n- Make repeated left/right corrections stable.\n- Preserve sufficient responsiveness for moving-target tracking.\n\nImplement a continuous, monotonic nonlinear response curve.\n\nAvoid abrupt response transitions.\n\n## 3. Progressive Steering\n\nSteering strength should increase progressively with mouse displacement from the virtual center.\n\nRequirements:\n\n- Low response near the center.\n- Gradually increasing response through the middle region.\n- Strong response near the outer region.\n- Independent yaw and pitch calibration.\n- Configurable maximum turn rates.\n\nDo not solve precision problems by globally reducing sensitivity.\n\n## 4. Reticle Boundary and Rebound\n\nRetain the rounded-rectangle reticle boundary and smooth self-centering behavior.\n\nRequirements:\n\n- The reticle must not instantly snap to the center.\n- Rebound should feel smooth and predictable.\n- Tune rebound speed and damping to avoid distracting oscillation.\n- The reticle must remain visually constrained.\n- Rebound animation must not accidentally reset or cancel ship steering.\n- Reaching the boundary must not create sudden steering spikes.\n\nThe reticle\'s visual position and steering demand must remain separate state variables.\n\n## 5. Angular Velocity and Damping\n\nReview how steering demand becomes ship rotation.\n\nRequirements:\n\n- Avoid excessive angular inertia.\n- Avoid sluggish acceleration.\n- Reduce unwanted rotation after input stops.\n- Support smooth directional reversal.\n- Prevent oscillation during fine corrections.\n\nUse separate controls for steering response and angular damping.\n\nDo not add unnecessary smoothing.\n\n## 6. Camera Coordination\n\nInspect the existing chase camera.\n\nEnsure:\n\n- Camera response is coordinated with ship rotation.\n- No excessive follow lag.\n- No camera motion that makes the reticle appear unstable.\n- Preserve the existing camera architecture unless a specific defect is identified.\n\n## 7. Settings and Calibration\n\nOrganize related tuning values in a centralized configuration.\n\nExpose useful settings where appropriate:\n\n- Base sensitivity.\n- Response curve.\n- Maximum yaw rate.\n- Maximum pitch rate.\n- Rebound speed.\n- Rebound damping.\n- Angular damping.\n- Yaw/pitch balance.\n\nUse sensible initial values based on the existing implementation and measured behavior.\n\nDo not claim values come from commercial games unless verified.\n\nAvoid exposing unnecessary technical controls in the player-facing Settings panel.\n\n## 8. Performance and Code Quality\n\n- No React state updates every frame for flight physics.\n- Use refs or engine state for real-time updates.\n- Avoid unnecessary allocations in the frame loop.\n- Keep input, steering, reticle, camera, and rendering responsibilities separated.\n- Preserve existing combat, weapons, bots, missions, and progression systems.\n\n## 9. Required Testing\n\nRun automated tests and actual browser gameplay tests where possible.\n\nTest all of the following:\n\n1. Tiny mouse corrections around the center.\n2. Slow tracking of a moving enemy.\n3. Repeated small corrections without overshooting.\n4. Approaching the reticle boundary.\n5. Smooth reticle rebound.\n6. Continuous steering during and after rebound.\n7. Large directional turns.\n8. Rapid left/right reversals.\n9. Stopping mouse input.\n10. Horizontal and vertical aiming independently.\n11. 30, 60, and 120 FPS behavior.\n\nPay particular attention to the interaction between rebound and steering demand.\n\n## 10. Acceptance Criteria\n\nThe optimization is complete only when:\n\n- Fine aiming is substantially easier.\n- Moving-target tracking feels stable.\n- Reticle rebound is smooth.\n- Rebound never interrupts steering.\n- Large turns remain fast.\n- Small corrections do not cause excessive rotation.\n- Yaw and pitch feel consistent.\n- No physical mouse-edge input interruption.\n- No regressions to existing gameplay systems.\n\n## Execution Instructions\n\nInspect the current implementation first.\n\nThen implement the complete related optimization in one coordinated pass.\n\nYou are authorized to inspect and edit relevant project files, run tests, and use browser testing tools available in the environment.\n\nDo not rewrite the entire game or replace the flight architecture.\n\nPreserve the rolled-back baseline so the complete optimization can be reverted.\n\nAt completion, report:\n\n- Root causes identified.\n- Files changed.\n- Control model implemented.\n- Exact final tuning values.\n- Automated test results.\n- Actual browser playtest results.\n- Known limitations.\n\nNever describe simulated tests as real mouse gameplay validation."
